WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.904 --> 00:00:04.571
(muddled electronic clicks)

00:00:28.001 --> 00:00:30.309
- Flash sonar is a way of,

00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:31.809
seeing with sound.

00:00:40.568 --> 00:00:43.401
(electric clicks)

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.050
You're esonifying the environment

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.936
with flashes of sound

00:00:50.936 --> 00:00:53.721
that would be a bit like
illuminating the environment

00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:55.888
with dim flashes of light.

00:00:58.737 --> 00:01:01.737
When that sound encounters surfaces,

00:01:03.138 --> 00:01:06.312
it reflects from those surfaces,

00:01:06.312 --> 00:01:10.019
and returns to the listener impressions,

00:01:10.019 --> 00:01:13.436
imprints, if you will, of those surfaces.

00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:19.028
In a very real sense,
it is a way of seeing,

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:23.170
but it is an image that is constructed

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.087
from elements of sound.

00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:35.073
(electronic static)

00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:54.970
(electronic mumbling)

00:02:10.315 --> 00:02:14.482
I speculate on how things
might have been different.

00:02:18.247 --> 00:02:20.162
Gosh, you know, in some parallel universe,

00:02:20.162 --> 00:02:22.842
maybe Daniel Kish didn't lose his vision

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.876
at the age of 13 months.

00:02:24.876 --> 00:02:27.253
And who is he, and what is he doing?

00:02:27.253 --> 00:02:30.586
He certainly isn't doing what I'm doing.

00:02:31.673 --> 00:02:35.006
(flat electronic tones)

00:02:36.566 --> 00:02:39.342
My parents did not see the blindness

00:02:39.342 --> 00:02:41.686
as an overriding factor.

00:02:41.686 --> 00:02:44.308
They definitely saw it as very secondary

00:02:44.308 --> 00:02:46.808
to my development as a person.

00:02:48.938 --> 00:02:51.188
(clicking)

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:57.299
They decided that I would need to grow up,

00:02:58.947 --> 00:03:00.489
like other kids.

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:03.760
That I would need to be
exposed to the same freedoms,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.927
and responsibilities, and
opportunities as other kids.

00:03:08.987 --> 00:03:11.487
(cat purring)

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.879
In my earliest memories,
I'm already echolocating.

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:25.183
My earliest memories,
I'm already clicking.

00:03:25.183 --> 00:03:27.283
(reversing vehicle beeping)

00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:29.950
(soft clicking)

00:03:36.851 --> 00:03:39.591
I was tree climbing at a very early age.

00:03:39.591 --> 00:03:41.341
Trees, fences, walls.

00:03:43.115 --> 00:03:44.571
Slides.

00:03:44.571 --> 00:03:46.904
Anything that was climbable.

00:03:52.594 --> 00:03:54.508
And I did run into things.

00:03:54.508 --> 00:03:56.892
I mean, I probably ran into my first pole

00:03:56.892 --> 00:03:58.642
when I was like, six.

00:04:00.016 --> 00:04:01.617
You get distracted for a moment,

00:04:01.617 --> 00:04:04.700
and you find yourself kissing a pole.

00:04:06.222 --> 00:04:07.885
It's an emergency trip to the dentist,

00:04:07.885 --> 00:04:10.625
and getting the teeth fixed,

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:12.208
and that sort of thing.

00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:19.180
And now we know, by watching our students,

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.245
and by bat research and dolphin research,

00:04:22.245 --> 00:04:25.123
we know that echolocating
with the environment,

00:04:25.123 --> 00:04:27.653
using active signals such as clicking,

00:04:27.653 --> 00:04:29.878
is an interactive process.

00:04:29.878 --> 00:04:34.045
(slow electronic music)
(soft clicking)

00:04:36.504 --> 00:04:40.671
It's like having a conversation
with the environment.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.719
Every click is essentially
asking the question,

00:04:48.719 --> 00:04:51.302
what are you and where are you?

00:04:54.965 --> 00:04:57.798
(mechanical boom)

00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:03.962
A fence, for example, will sound sparse,

00:05:03.962 --> 00:05:06.629
whereas a wall will sound solid.

00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:11.287
So, as you want more information,

00:05:11.287 --> 00:05:13.538
you start to vary the
frequency of your clicks,

00:05:13.538 --> 00:05:16.038
and the quality of the clicks.

00:05:18.363 --> 00:05:21.979
So it becomes a kind
of language of inquiry

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:25.562
to gather information
from the environment.

00:05:29.456 --> 00:05:32.956
Essentially, that's how flash sonar works.

00:05:47.197 --> 00:05:48.030
(clicking)

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.376
Okay, so.

00:05:49.376 --> 00:05:52.145
We'll just do this quick and easy.

00:05:52.145 --> 00:05:54.395
Can you tell me which side?

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:58.246
(clicking)

00:05:58.246 --> 00:05:59.079
- My left.

00:05:59.079 --> 00:06:00.397
- Can you touch it?

00:06:00.397 --> 00:06:02.325
Bing, thank you.

00:06:02.325 --> 00:06:03.158
And,

00:06:04.213 --> 00:06:05.321
now?

00:06:05.321 --> 00:06:07.571
(clicking)

00:06:11.902 --> 00:06:12.735
- My right.

00:06:12.735 --> 00:06:15.338
- Okay, now localize where it is,

00:06:15.338 --> 00:06:18.126
and then reach out and touch it.

00:06:18.126 --> 00:06:19.656
There, whew!

00:06:19.656 --> 00:06:21.792
Well done, beautiful.

00:06:21.792 --> 00:06:24.042
(clicking)

00:06:30.661 --> 00:06:31.494
Yep.

00:06:31.494 --> 00:06:33.073
(synthetic click)

00:06:33.073 --> 00:06:34.897
I love your power click, by the way.

00:06:34.897 --> 00:06:37.523
- Oh, I had a habit of
doing that since I was six.

00:06:37.523 --> 00:06:39.078
- That's awesome!

00:06:39.078 --> 00:06:41.747
(synthetic click)

00:06:41.747 --> 00:06:43.247
Okay, now look up,

00:06:44.083 --> 00:06:46.775
and then kind of move to your right,

00:06:46.775 --> 00:06:48.864
and see what you notice about this tree.

00:06:48.864 --> 00:06:50.349
You may remember it visually,

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:52.397
but see what you can hear about this tree.

00:06:52.397 --> 00:06:53.816
(clicking)

00:06:53.816 --> 00:06:54.649
- It curves.

00:06:54.649 --> 00:06:56.268
- It curves, yeah!

00:06:56.268 --> 00:07:00.723
See if you can find where
they kind of come down,

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:01.731
there's a point where they kind of--

00:07:01.731 --> 00:07:03.889
- The base, the base of the trunk.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:04.746
- Yep.
- Like where it's all--

00:07:04.746 --> 00:07:06.860
- Okay, oh, oh, right, you found it, yep.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.443
What we try to do with
World Access for the Blind,

00:07:09.443 --> 00:07:13.276
is help our students to
develop an environment

00:07:16.507 --> 00:07:18.840
that supports their freedom.

00:07:22.359 --> 00:07:25.093
And then one thing that
I often tell students,

00:07:25.093 --> 00:07:27.408
and this is just sort of, (sighs)

00:07:27.408 --> 00:07:30.491
I mean, everyone has their own style,

00:07:31.382 --> 00:07:35.549
but you may find that if you
click a bit less frequently.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:38.904
- (clicking) Yeah.

00:07:38.904 --> 00:07:40.451
- You know, and let,

00:07:40.451 --> 00:07:42.267
I like to say,

00:07:42.267 --> 00:07:46.140
to let, to let yourself breathe between clicks.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.988
- Okay.
- You may find that,

00:07:48.988 --> 00:07:52.843
for one thing, you may
find your brain tires less,

00:07:52.843 --> 00:07:56.426
because you can devote
more processing time

00:07:57.277 --> 00:07:58.444
to each click.

00:08:00.673 --> 00:08:01.861
- An overhang.

00:08:01.861 --> 00:08:04.053
- More overhanging trees.

00:08:04.053 --> 00:08:05.862
- I outta carry a machete next time.

00:08:05.862 --> 00:08:06.695
- Yes.

00:08:07.809 --> 00:08:09.505
I'm with you on that.

00:08:09.505 --> 00:08:13.020
It isn't all just about
throwing skills at a student.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.187
It's about facilitating the
development of a culture

00:08:18.291 --> 00:08:22.124
that supports the
development of those skills.

00:08:30.589 --> 00:08:33.839
(mechanical thrumming)

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:48.148
I am blind as a result of retinoblastoma,

00:08:48.148 --> 00:08:50.231
which are retinal tumors,

00:08:51.195 --> 00:08:54.149
and I lost my first eye at
the age of seven months,

00:08:54.149 --> 00:08:57.316
and my second at the age of 13 months.

00:08:59.443 --> 00:09:02.026
These are both prosthetic eyes.

00:09:10.107 --> 00:09:12.357
(clicking)

00:09:34.476 --> 00:09:36.811
(cat meowing)

00:09:36.811 --> 00:09:37.644
Okay.

00:09:40.072 --> 00:09:42.489
(cat mewing)

00:09:43.426 --> 00:09:45.351
We have to remember that sighted children

00:09:45.351 --> 00:09:47.101
have to learn to see.

00:09:48.751 --> 00:09:52.586
You don't pop out of the
womb knowing how to see.

00:09:52.586 --> 00:09:54.130
You pop out of the womb

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.277
with a predisposition to learning to see,

00:09:58.277 --> 00:10:02.444
but that learning process
occurs through experience.

00:10:03.651 --> 00:10:05.651
(click)

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.884
And it happened rather
quickly and rather naturally,

00:10:09.884 --> 00:10:12.394
because sighted society supports,

00:10:12.394 --> 00:10:14.825
sighted children to learn to see,

00:10:14.825 --> 00:10:18.997
sighted children to
learn to use their senses

00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:22.783
to govern their interaction
with their environment,

00:10:22.783 --> 00:10:24.366
on their own terms.

00:10:26.412 --> 00:10:29.193
For myself, the process was very similar,

00:10:29.193 --> 00:10:31.180
and I don't remember it.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.226
I developed my capacity to echolocate

00:10:34.226 --> 00:10:38.000
and to find my way around the environment

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.639
as a fairly natural function
of being expected to do so,

00:10:41.639 --> 00:10:43.073
and supported to do so,

00:10:43.073 --> 00:10:46.485
and mostly people not getting in my way.

00:10:46.485 --> 00:10:49.068
(dogs barking)

00:10:50.604 --> 00:10:53.502
I have a cat that could
eat both of you for dinner.

00:10:53.502 --> 00:10:54.825
(man laughing)

00:10:54.825 --> 00:10:57.075
(clicking)

00:10:57.964 --> 00:11:00.464
(dog barking)

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.317
And you, too, probably.

00:11:07.704 --> 00:11:09.826
But looking back, I can say that,

00:11:09.826 --> 00:11:11.993
while running into a pole,

00:11:13.187 --> 00:11:14.020
is a drag.

00:11:14.979 --> 00:11:18.885
Never being allowed to run into a pole

00:11:18.885 --> 00:11:19.968
is a tragedy.

00:11:21.545 --> 00:11:25.712
(birds singing)
(smooth resonant music)

00:11:26.651 --> 00:11:27.938
'Cause what you're really talking about

00:11:27.938 --> 00:11:29.855
is basic human freedom.

00:11:33.998 --> 00:11:37.033
In most places, blind kids
aren't even given any kind

00:11:37.033 --> 00:11:39.095
of cane training or mobility training

00:11:39.095 --> 00:11:42.678
until they're seven,
eight, nine years old.

00:11:44.332 --> 00:11:48.647
It's thought that a blind
child isn't responsible enough

00:11:48.647 --> 00:11:52.097
to have this freedom, to be
able to make responsible use

00:11:52.097 --> 00:11:53.743
of this freedom.

00:11:53.743 --> 00:11:55.674
It's easier to guide them around,

00:11:55.674 --> 00:11:59.341
it's easier to keep
them relatively passive.

00:12:00.661 --> 00:12:03.506
Blind children kept inside,

00:12:03.506 --> 00:12:05.546
out of the light of day,

00:12:05.546 --> 00:12:07.230
out of society,

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:11.044
not allowed to mix, not allowed to mingle,

00:12:11.044 --> 00:12:13.961
not allowed to be a part of things,

00:12:18.142 --> 00:12:20.876
and maybe, we could argue that it's,

00:12:20.876 --> 00:12:23.441
in their best interest, quote unquote,

00:12:23.441 --> 00:12:25.398
but is it really?

00:12:25.398 --> 00:12:27.898
Does it really come from that?

00:12:28.929 --> 00:12:31.421
Or does it come, at least to some degree,

00:12:31.421 --> 00:12:33.504
from our own discomforts,

00:12:36.009 --> 00:12:38.092
which we can't reconcile?

00:12:47.461 --> 00:12:50.628
(electronic clicking)

00:13:01.721 --> 00:13:04.244
(Daniel clicking)

00:13:04.244 --> 00:13:05.077
Oh, whoa.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:11.993
Ah!

00:13:16.104 --> 00:13:19.480
(clicking) Daniel Boone
said something like,

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.908
I've never been lost,

00:13:21.908 --> 00:13:24.075
just momentarily confused.

00:13:31.805 --> 00:13:35.388
We're sometimes programmed
to feel the bite

00:13:36.653 --> 00:13:39.403
of anxiety before it's necessary.

00:13:43.768 --> 00:13:46.851
Society often programs us to do that.

00:13:48.676 --> 00:13:52.259
It programs us to be
afraid of the unknown.

00:13:54.999 --> 00:13:57.051
It doesn't really help to
be afraid of the unknown

00:13:57.051 --> 00:13:58.384
if you're blind.

00:14:02.583 --> 00:14:03.906
And once you get over that fear,

00:14:03.906 --> 00:14:05.912
you can pretty much master anything.

00:14:05.912 --> 00:14:10.079
(clicking)
(soft dripping music)

00:14:59.681 --> 00:15:03.014
(bright trilling music)

00:15:06.655 --> 00:15:09.238
(ambient tone)

